//? Title: Bidirectional Search
//? Subtitle: Searching from both ends: the stopping rules that make it exact, how much it saves as a function of how fast balls grow, and measurements on a handwritten-digit graph and the Maunga Whau volcano
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Bidirectional search finds a shortest path by growing one search from the start and one from the goal until they meet. We derive the stopping rules that make it exact: finish the whole layer in breadth-first search, and stop when the heap tops sum to the best path seen in Dijkstra's algorithm. Stopping at the first contact is wrong, and the library's earlier implementation, which did so, returned invalid paths for 9% of random graphs. How much the method saves depends on how fast balls grow: the exponent is halved when growth is exponential, but the saving is only a constant \(2^{D-1}\) when growth is polynomial of degree \(D\). On a 10-nearest-neighbour graph of 1,797 handwritten digits, where balls grow by a factor of 15 then 5 per hop, bidirectional BFS does 4 to 20 times less work, and a ball-growth model predicts it. On the Maunga Whau volcano, a planar grid, the saving for short routes is the predicted factor 2. For routes across the grid it falls to about 1, because the map edge clips the single large search.
)

\toc

> The idea

To find a shortest path from \(s\) to \(t\), breadth-first search and Dijkstra's algorithm grow a
ball around \(s\) until it reaches \(t\). *Bidirectional search* (Pohl 1971) grows two balls at once,
one around \(s\) along the edges and one around \(t\) along the reversed edges, and stops when they meet.
Each ball only needs radius about \(d/2\), where \(d\) is the distance from \(s\) to \(t\).

How much that saves depends on how ball sizes grow with radius. Let \(|B(r)|\) be the number of
vertices within distance \(r\).

- **Exponential growth**, \(|B(r)|\approx b^r\), as in a tree with branching factor \(b\). One ball of
  radius \(d\) costs \(b^d\), and two of radius \(d/2\) cost \(2b^{d/2}\). The saving is \(b^{d/2}/2\), so
  the exponent is halved.
- **Polynomial growth**, \(|B(r)|\approx cr^D\), as in a \(D\)-dimensional grid or road network. The
  ratio is \(c\,d^D/\big(2c(d/2)^D\big)=2^{D-1}\), a constant: 2 in the plane and 4 in space,
  whatever the distance.

The method does nothing about the work inside each ball. It only exploits the gap between one
large ball and two small ones, so that gap is what the measurements below look at.

> Stopping correctly

The searches must not simply stop the first time they touch.

>> Unweighted graphs

`algorithms::bidirectional_bfs` grows the side with the smaller frontier by one *complete* layer at
a time. While expanding a layer it records every edge \(u\to v\) whose end \(v\) is already labelled by
the other side, which offers a path of length \(d_s(u)+1+d_t(v)\). It stops after the first layer that
finds any such edge and returns the best one.

Why the whole layer? Suppose the forward side has completed layers up to depth \(k\) and the backward
side up to \(j\), and none has met. Any path of length \(L\le k+j\) contains a vertex with
\(d_s(v)\le k\) *and* \(d_t(v)\le j\): take the vertex \(\min(k,L)\) edges along it. That vertex would
already carry both labels. So the shortest path has length at least \(k+j+1\). Expanding layer \(k+1\)
finds every path of length exactly \(k+j+1\): each passes through an edge from forward layer \(k\) to a
vertex at backward depth \(j\). The first edge found in the layer need not be the best, since it may
reach a backward vertex at depth \(j\) while another edge reaches one at depth \(j-1\). That is why the
layer is finished and the minimum taken.

The rule matters in practice. The library's earlier `bidirectional_search` shared one queue between
the two sides and stopped at the first edge that touched. On 3,000 random graphs it returned a path
that was not valid in 9% of cases. It now calls `bidirectional_bfs`, and the tests check it against
plain BFS on 3,000 random graphs, directed and undirected.

>> Weighted graphs

`algorithms::bidirectional_dijkstra` runs Dijkstra forward from \(s\) and backward from \(t\), each
time advancing the side whose heap top \(k_s\) or \(k_t\) is smaller. Whenever it scans an edge
\(u\to v\) with weight \(w\) and \(v\) has a label on the other side, it updates

$$
\mu=\min\big(\mu,\;d_s(u)+w+d_t(v)\big).
$$
\alttext(mu equals the minimum of mu and d s of u plus w plus d t of v.)

It stops as soon as \(k_s+k_t\ge\mu\). The proof is a one-liner. A path not yet seen joins a vertex
unsettled on the forward side to one unsettled on the backward side, so its length is at least
\(k_s+k_t\ge\mu\).

The natural alternative, stopping at the first vertex settled by both sides and returning the path
through it, is wrong. Take \(s\) and \(t\) joined directly by an edge of length 5, and also through \(x\)
by two edges of length 3. Both searches settle \(x\) at distance 3 before either settles the other
terminal at 5, so \(x\) is the first vertex settled twice, and the path through it costs 6. The rule
above finds the direct edge, because scanning \(s\)'s edges already set \(\mu=5\). One of the library
tests encodes this example.

> A graph with fast growth: handwritten digits

`dm::datasets::digits()` holds 1,797 images of handwritten digits as 64 pixel intensities. We join
every image to its 10 nearest neighbours (`geometry::knn_lists`) and ignore edge direction. The
result is a sparse graph in which a few hops connect any image to similar ones, and longer paths
cross between digits. We measure ball growth from 50 random centres and run plain BFS and
bidirectional BFS between 400 random pairs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bidir_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::size_t n = digits.nrows();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> x(n, std::vector<double>(64));
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto column = digits.get_column("pixel_" + std::to_string(j)).values();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) x[i][j] = column[i];
  }
  std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, std::size_t>> edges;
  const auto lists = dm::geometry::knn_lists(x, 10);
  for (std::size_t u = 0; u < n; ++u)
    for (int v : lists[u]) edges.emplace_back(u, v);

  std::mt19937_64 rng(1971);
  std::uniform_int_distribution<std::size_t> pick(0, n - 1);
  const std::size_t max_r = 12;
  std::vector<double> mean_ball(max_r + 1, 0.0);
  for (int c = 0; c < 50; ++c) {
    const auto b = alg::ball_sizes(n, edges, pick(rng), max_r);
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r <= max_r; ++r) mean_ball[r] += b[r] / 50.0;
  }
  std::print("Mean ball size \\(|B(r)|\\) over 50 centres, and growth factor \\(|B(r)|/|B(r-1)|\\):\n\n| \\(r\\) |");
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r <= 10; ++r) std::print(" {} |", r);
  std::print("\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| \\(|B(r)|\\) |");
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r <= 10; ++r) std::print(" {:.0f} |", mean_ball[r]);
  std::print("\n| growth |  |");
  for (std::size_t r = 1; r <= 10; ++r) std::print(" {:.2f} |", mean_ball[r] / mean_ball[r - 1]);
  std::print("\n\n");

  struct Bucket { double count = 0, one = 0, two = 0; };
  std::vector<Bucket> by_distance(20);
  std::vector<double> dx, ratio;
  std::size_t mismatches = 0, reachable = 0;
  for (int p = 0; p < 400; ++p) {
    const std::size_t s = pick(rng), t = pick(rng);
    const auto one = alg::bfs_pair_search(n, edges, s, t);
    const auto two = alg::bidirectional_bfs(n, edges, s, t);
    mismatches += one.found != two.found || (one.found && one.distance != two.distance);
    if (!one.found || s == t) continue;
    ++reachable;
    auto& b = by_distance[static_cast<std::size_t>(one.distance)];
    b.count += 1, b.one += one.expanded(), b.two += two.expanded();
    dx.push_back(one.distance + std::uniform_real_distribution<double>(-0.2, 0.2)(rng));
    ratio.push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(one.expanded()) / two.expanded()));
  }
  std::print("{} of 400 pairs are connected; bidirectional and plain BFS disagree on the distance in {} pairs.\n\n"
             "| distance \\(d\\) | pairs | BFS: vertices expanded | bidirectional | ratio | model BFS | model bidirectional | model ratio |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n", reachable, mismatches);
  for (std::size_t d = 1; d < by_distance.size(); ++d) {
    const auto& b = by_distance[d];
    if (b.count < 3) continue;
    // Model: BFS expands B(d-1) and about half of layer d; the bidirectional search expands
    // B(a) on one side and B(b-1) on the other with a + b = d - 1, split as evenly as is cheapest.
    const auto ball = [&](long r) { return r < 0 ? 0.0 : mean_ball[static_cast<std::size_t>(r)]; };
    const double one_model = (ball(static_cast<long>(d) - 1) + ball(static_cast<long>(d))) / 2;
    double two_model = 1e300;
    for (long a = 0; a <= static_cast<long>(d) - 1; ++a) two_model = std::min(two_model, ball(a) + ball(static_cast<long>(d) - 2 - a));
    std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.1f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.1f} |\n", d, b.count, b.one / b.count,
               b.two / b.count, b.one / b.two, one_model, two_model, one_model / two_model);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(dx, ratio, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3.0)
      .abline_h(0, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0)
      .y_limits(-0.5, 2.5)
      .title("Digit graph: work saved by searching from both ends")
      .x_label("shortest-path distance (hops), jittered")
      .y_label("log10 (BFS work / bidirectional work)")
      .size(760, 440)
      .save_svg("build/bidir_digits.svg");
}
```
