\image(figures/bidir_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(For 400 random pairs in the digit graph: the ratio of vertices expanded by plain BFS to those expanded by bidirectional BFS, on a log scale, against the distance between the pair.)
\alttext(Scatter plot of log10 work ratio against path distance from 1 to 11 hops. All 400 points lie above zero. At distances 1 to 4 most ratios are between 10 and 30 (log 1 to 1.5); the ratios fall steadily with distance, to about 4 to 6 (log 0.6 to 0.8) at distances 8 to 11, with less scatter.)

The digit graph grows very fast near each vertex. One hop reaches 14 neighbours on average, because every image keeps its own 10 nearest and is also kept by others. Two hops reach 71 and three 183. The growth factor then falls as balls start to fill the 1,797 vertices, and ten hops reach almost everything. This is the exponential regime, cut short by the finite graph, and bidirectional search pays off accordingly. Both searches agree on every distance, and the bidirectional one expands 11 to 20 times fewer vertices for pairs 2 to 4 hops apart, and still 4 to 5 times fewer at 9 to 10 hops.

The model columns predict the work from the mean ball sizes alone. BFS expands everything closer than the goal and, on average, half of the goal's layer. The bidirectional search expands \(B(a)\) around one end and \(B(b-1)\) around the other with \(a+b=d-1\). The model tracks the measurements up to 8 hops. For more distant pairs the measured saving is larger than the model predicts. Pairs that far apart lie at the edge of the graph, where balls are smaller than average, and always expanding the smaller frontier exploits the side that grows slowly. The model also explains why the ratio falls with distance. The saving \(|B(d)|/2|B(d/2)|\) needs \(B(d)\) to keep growing, and here it stops growing at the size of the graph.

> A graph with slow growth: walking on a volcano

`dm::datasets::volcano()` gives the heights of Maunga Whau on an \(87\times61\) grid with 10 m
spacing. `algorithms::terrain_from_frame` and `terrain_edges` turn it into a directed graph
with an edge to each of the 8 neighbouring cells. An edge costs its length plus 8.3 m for every
metre climbed (Naismith's rule), so climbing costs more than descending and the graph really is
directed. This is a planar graph, so \(D=2\) and the predicted saving is about 2.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bidir_volcano.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto volcano = dm::datasets::volcano();
  const auto grid = alg::terrain_from_frame(volcano, "x", "y", "elevation", 8.3);
  const auto edges = alg::terrain_edges(grid);
  const std::size_t n = grid.rows * grid.cols;

  // One route across the cone, south-east foot to north-west foot.
  const std::size_t start = 80 * grid.cols + 5, goal = 5 * grid.cols + 55;
  const auto one = alg::dijkstra_pair_search(n, edges, start, goal);
  const auto two = alg::bidirectional_dijkstra(n, edges, start, goal);
  std::print("| search | cost | vertices settled | forward | backward | edges scanned |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n"
             "| Dijkstra | {:.1f} | {} | {} | – | {} |\n| bidirectional Dijkstra | {:.1f} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n\n",
             one.distance, one.expanded(), one.forward_expanded, one.edges_scanned, two.distance, two.expanded(),
             two.forward_expanded, two.backward_expanded, two.edges_scanned);

  // Which cells each search settles: rerun the searches' frontiers via cost-from / cost-to-go maps.
  const auto from_start = alg::terrain_cost_from(grid, start);
  const auto to_goal = alg::terrain_cost_to_go(grid, goal);
  double forward_radius = 0, backward_radius = 0;
  {
    std::vector<double> f = from_start, b = to_goal;
    std::ranges::sort(f);
    std::ranges::sort(b);
    forward_radius = f[two.forward_expanded - 1];
    backward_radius = b[two.backward_expanded - 1];
  }
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> category(grid.rows, std::vector<double>(grid.cols, 0.0));
  std::size_t uni = 0;
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < n; ++c) {
    double v = 0;
    if (from_start[c] <= one.distance) v = 1, ++uni;
    if (from_start[c] <= forward_radius) v = 2;
    if (to_goal[c] <= backward_radius) v = 3;
    category[c / grid.cols][c % grid.cols] = v;
  }
  std::print("Settled cells are those within cost {:.1f} of the start for Dijkstra ({} cells), and within "
             "{:.1f} of the start or {:.1f} of the goal for the bidirectional search; {:.1f} + {:.1f} = {:.1f} "
             "\\(\\ge\\) the optimum.\n\n", one.distance, uni, forward_radius, backward_radius, forward_radius,
             backward_radius, forward_radius + backward_radius);

  // Many random pairs.
  std::mt19937_64 rng(1959);
  std::uniform_int_distribution<std::size_t> pick(0, n - 1);
  std::vector<double> dist, ratio;
  double total_one = 0, total_two = 0;
  for (int p = 0; p < 300; ++p) {
    const std::size_t s = pick(rng), t = pick(rng);
    if (s == t) continue;
    const auto a = alg::dijkstra_pair_search(n, edges, s, t);
    const auto b = alg::bidirectional_dijkstra(n, edges, s, t);
    if (std::abs(a.distance - b.distance) > 1e-9 * a.distance) std::print("MISMATCH {} {}\n", s, t);
    dist.push_back(a.distance);
    ratio.push_back(static_cast<double>(a.expanded()) / b.expanded());
    total_one += a.expanded(), total_two += b.expanded();
  }
  const auto band = [&](double lo, double hi) {
    std::vector<double> out;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < ratio.size(); ++i)
      if (dist[i] >= lo && dist[i] < hi) out.push_back(ratio[i]);
    return out;
  };
  const auto median = [](std::vector<double> v) {
    std::ranges::sort(v);
    return v[v.size() / 2];
  };
  std::vector<double> sorted = ratio;
  std::ranges::sort(sorted);
  std::print("Over {} random pairs, all with equal costs from both searches: Dijkstra settles {:.0f} cells on "
             "average and bidirectional Dijkstra {:.0f}, a ratio of {:.2f}. Per pair, the ratio has median {:.2f} "
             "and quartiles {:.2f} and {:.2f}; it is below 1 for {} pairs. For the {} pairs with optimal cost under 300 the "
             "median ratio is {:.2f}, and for the {} above 900 it is {:.2f}.\n", ratio.size(), total_one / ratio.size(),
             total_two / ratio.size(), total_one / total_two, sorted[sorted.size() / 2], sorted[sorted.size() / 4],
             sorted[3 * sorted.size() / 4], std::ranges::count_if(ratio, [](double r) { return r < 1; }),
             band(0, 300).size(), median(band(0, 300)), band(900, 1e9).size(), median(band(900, 1e9)));

  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < grid.rows; ++i) xs.push_back(grid.cell_size * i);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.cols; ++j) ys.push_back(grid.cell_size * j);
  std::vector<double> px, py;
  for (std::size_t c : two.path) px.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c / grid.cols)), py.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c % grid.cols));
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> palette = {{241, 245, 249}, {203, 213, 225}, {147, 197, 253}, {253, 186, 116}};
  auto map = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .image(xs, ys, category, palette, 0, 3)
                 .lines(px, py, "", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 2.0)
                 .legend({"Dijkstra only", "forward", "backward"}, {palette[1], palette[2], palette[3]})
                 .y_limits(0, 700)
                 .title("Cells settled on one route")
                 .x_label("x (m)")
                 .y_label("y (m)");
  auto scatter = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                     .points(dist, ratio, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3.0)
                     .abline_h(2, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0)
                     .abline_h(1, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0)
                     .y_limits(0, 4)
                     .title("300 random pairs (red line: 2)")
                     .x_label("optimal cost")
                     .y_label("Dijkstra work / bidirectional work");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(map).add(scatter).size(1300, 480).save_svg("build/bidir_volcano.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| search | cost | vertices settled | forward | backward | edges scanned |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Dijkstra | 1290.9 | 4449 | 4449 | – | 34807 |
| bidirectional Dijkstra | 1290.9 | 4508 | 1837 | 2671 | 35332 |

Settled cells are those within cost 1290.9 of the start for Dijkstra (4449 cells), and within 645.3 of the start or 645.3 of the goal for the bidirectional search; 645.3 + 645.3 = 1290.6 \(\ge\) the optimum.

Over 300 random pairs, all with equal costs from both searches: Dijkstra settles 2554 cells on average and bidirectional Dijkstra 1964, a ratio of 1.30. Per pair, the ratio has median 1.31 and quartiles 1.09 and 1.80; it is below 1 for 47 pairs. For the 64 pairs with optimal cost under 300 the median ratio is 2.04, and for the 61 above 900 it is 1.15.
\image(figures/bidir_volcano.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: cells settled on the route from the south-east to the north-west foot of the volcano. Grey cells are settled only by one-directional Dijkstra, blue by the forward half and orange by the backward half of the bidirectional search; the black line is the optimal route. Right: the ratio of cells settled by the two searches for 300 random pairs, against the cost of the optimal route.)
\alttext(Two panels. Left: a map of the 870 by 610 metre grid. A blue region, cells settled by the forward search, covers the right third of the map around the start at the bottom right. An orange region, cells settled by the backward search, covers the upper left around the goal. A grey band between them, running diagonally from the bottom middle to the top right, marks cells that only one-directional Dijkstra settles. The optimal route, a black line, runs north from the start, then north-west, along the northern edge to the goal, skirting the summit. Right: a scatter of work ratio against optimal cost from 0 to 1500. For costs under 300 the ratios spread between about 1 and 3.3, centred near 2; as cost grows they fall towards 1, and some pairs lie below 1, down to about 0.7.)

On the route across the cone the two searches find the same cost, 1,290.9, but the bidirectional one settles slightly *more* cells: 4,508 against 4,449. Alternating by the smaller heap top kept both radii at 645.3, half the optimum, as the stopping rule requires. The two half-cost regions are not smaller than the one full-cost region, because the full-cost region is clipped by the map. A one-directional search to cost 1,290.9 already covers 84% of the 5,307 cells, so it cannot grow any further, while the two half-searches have room to grow into corners that the route never visits.

Over 300 random pairs, bidirectional Dijkstra saves 23% of the work on average (a ratio of 1.30). The ratio depends on the route length, as the planar model predicts. For the 64 pairs with optimal cost under 300, whose searches stay inside the map, the median ratio is 2.04, exactly the \(2^{D-1}\) of a two-dimensional graph. For the 61 pairs above 900 it is 1.15, and in 47 pairs the bidirectional search does more work. Uphill costs make the regions of equal cost lopsided, which probably explains why the ratios for short routes spread between 1 and 3.

> Summary

- Searching from both ends replaces one ball of radius \(d\) by two of radius \(d/2\). With exponential growth \(b^r\) the work drops from \(b^d\) to \(2b^{d/2}\). With polynomial growth \(r^D\) the saving is a constant \(2^{D-1}\).
- Exactness needs the right stopping rule. BFS must finish the layer in which the frontiers first touch, and Dijkstra must continue until \(k_s+k_t\ge\mu\), returning the best path \(\mu\) seen across any scanned edge, not the path through the first vertex settled twice. The library's earlier `bidirectional_search` stopped at the first contact and returned invalid paths for 9% of random graphs. It now uses `bidirectional_bfs`.
- On the digit graph, where balls grow 15-fold and then 5-fold per hop, bidirectional BFS expands 4 to 20 times fewer vertices, and a model built only from mean ball sizes predicts the saving.
- On the volcano grid the saving is the planar factor 2 for routes that stay inside the map. It disappears for routes across it, because the map boundary limits the single large search more than the two small ones.
- `algorithms::bidirectional_bfs`, `bidirectional_dijkstra`, the baselines `bfs_pair_search` and `dijkstra_pair_search`, and `ball_sizes` are in `<datamunge/algorithms/bidirectional_search.hpp>`. All of them report their work counters.

> References

- Pohl, I. (1971). Bi-directional search. *Machine Intelligence* 6, 127–140.
- Goldberg, A. V. and Harrelson, C. (2005). Computing the shortest path: A* search meets graph
  theory. *Proceedings of the 16th ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms*, 156–165.
- Holte, R. C., Felner, A., Sharon, G. and Sturtevant, N. R. (2016). Bidirectional search that is
  guaranteed to meet in the middle. *Proceedings of AAAI-16*, 3411–3417.
- Russell, S. and Norvig, P. (2021). *Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach*, 4th edition,
  Section 3.4.5. Pearson.
